    ("h1", "3 System Model and Problem"),
    ("h2", "3.1 Network and channels"),
    ("p", "The network has n = 20 nodes placed at random in a square of side 30√n m, with two gateways. A link exists when the received power is at least −76 dBm, and two links interfere at −82 dBm or more. Path loss follows the ns-3 default log-distance model [10], and rates follow 802.11ax MCS (8.6–143.4 Mb/s). Every node has two radios: the downlink radio picks one of six channels, and the uplink radio uses its parent's channel. Interfering links on the same channel form one domain, and the airtime actually used in a domain is capped at η = 0.65, a value calibrated against ns-3 (Section 6.6). Uplink traffic is on/off, a step is 100 ms and an episode is 200 steps. A node that switches channel or parent cannot use that link for one step, and in setting (a) the interrupted flows lose their queues."),
    ("h2", "3.2 Problem formulation"),
    ("p", "Every node is an agent in a partially observable Markov game. An action is a pair (downlink channel, parent slot), 24 in total. The system reward is R = Σ log(x̄_f + 0.01) − 0.1N_sw − 0.05N_rc − 0.1N_drop, where x̄_f is the 1-second average throughput of flow f. The per-agent reward is r_i = λu_i + (1 − λ)R/n − 0.1s_i − 0.05c_i. Baselines use λ = 0.5; the proposed method anneals λ from 0.5 to 0.1 during training."),
    ("h1", "4 EGAT-MAPPO"),
    ("p", "Fig. 1 gives an overview of the model; the dashed parts are used only during training. The pipeline has six stages: (1) node and edge features, (2) a shared GNN encoder, (3) route and channel heads, (4) the Ko residual prior, (5) decentralised execution and (6) the action of every node."),
    ("img", "fig_architecture.png", 6.5),
    ("cf", "EGAT-MAPPO architecture (n is the number of nodes, σ is LeakyReLU; green boxes and dashed arrows are used only during training)"),
    ("p", "The encoder is a two-layer GATv2 with four heads and hidden size 64. The attention score is s_ij = aᵀ LeakyReLU(W_s h_i + W_t h_j + W_e e_ij), where e_ij holds seven features: link, interference, SNR, rate, load, shared channel and delivery ratio. The node embedding goes straight to the decision heads, with no memory across steps. The route head decides every step and the channel head every 10 steps; a progress mask allows only parents that are closer to a gateway. Both heads subtract Ko's cost scaled by a learnable factor: logits − softplus(β)·z(cost_Ko)."),
    ("p", "Training runs for 100,000 steps. The first 10,000 steps are behaviour cloning from Ko for both the route and channel heads, followed by MAPPO (clip 0.2, GAE λ 0.95, rollout 1,000). The centralised critic is used only during training, and the checkpoint is selected by the composite on validation topologies. At execution, (1) channels are decoded sequentially from parents to children, as for every learned baseline; (2) switch hysteresis adds m to the logits of the current channel and parent, with m = 2 chosen from {0, 0.5, 1, 2, 3, 4} on validation by a worst-case rule; and (3) a node falls back to Ko's action when the summed entropy of its two heads exceeds 2 nats, which can happen only on channel steps and occurred in 0.02% of decisions. These rules apply only at execution. One decision step takes 10.6–32.1 ms at 20–80 nodes on a single CPU thread."),
